WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:03.103
-Our next guest is the
legendary Emmy Award-winning

00:00:03.103 --> 00:00:04.670
stand-up comedian and actor.

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:07.574
You can see her performing
Charles Dickens'

00:00:07.574 --> 00:00:10.710
"Great Expectations" at the
Greenwich Theater in New York.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:13.579
Please welcome back or friend,
Eddie Izzard, everyone.

00:00:13.579 --> 00:00:15.581
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:15.581 --> 00:00:29.128 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:29.128 --> 00:00:30.829
Welcome back, Eddie!

00:00:30.829 --> 00:00:32.264
-Very goodsies.

00:00:32.264 --> 00:00:33.800
I know I was here before.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:38.171
In the -- one of the
dinosaur eras sometime ago.

00:00:38.171 --> 00:00:39.472
-Yeah, it's been a while --

00:00:39.472 --> 00:00:40.607
-I like to space them out,

00:00:40.607 --> 00:00:41.774
Just so you know,
it's nothing personal.

00:00:41.774 --> 00:00:43.075
-You don't like to do
them too close together.

00:00:43.075 --> 00:00:44.811
-I know I could have been back
earlier but I said,

00:00:44.811 --> 00:00:48.548
No, let's make it long
so everyone says,

00:00:48.548 --> 00:00:50.049
well, who? Who is this?

00:00:50.049 --> 00:00:52.651
But it was back in one of the
dinosaur eras, and it was good.

00:00:52.651 --> 00:00:54.487
You had a great show then,
and it's a great show now.

00:00:54.487 --> 00:00:55.755
-Well, I'm very happy
to have you here.

00:00:55.755 --> 00:00:58.524
-I'm always a little
taken aback by

00:00:58.524 --> 00:01:01.394
the challenges you present
to yourself as a performer.

00:01:01.394 --> 00:01:04.230
Obviously I know you as
a very accomplished stand-up,

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:07.467
and yet here you are doing a
one-person show based on

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:09.735
Charles Dickens' "Great
Expectations."

00:01:09.735 --> 00:01:11.237
Is this something
you always wanted to do?

00:01:11.237 --> 00:01:14.440
-When I was 7 I wanted
to be an actor.

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:16.676
I don't know how comedy
attracted you

00:01:16.676 --> 00:01:19.111
when you were a kid, but it was
just acting, just acting.

00:01:19.111 --> 00:01:20.612
My mom died when I was a kid,
and I thought

00:01:20.612 --> 00:01:21.848
Oh, the applause of the
audience,

00:01:21.848 --> 00:01:23.249
I kind of craved that love.

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:26.018
And then -- I was a smaller kid.

00:01:26.018 --> 00:01:29.088
I was dyslexic so I couldn't do
sight readings for auditions,

00:01:29.088 --> 00:01:31.390
so I was the jailer or the
butler, or the thing.

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:32.692
Not the interesting one.

00:01:32.692 --> 00:01:34.661
I was once asked, what kind of
role would you like?

00:01:34.661 --> 00:01:37.063
And I said, well, you know those
big roles with the sword

00:01:37.063 --> 00:01:38.598
and you kill everyone and you
get the girl?

00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:39.999
That kind of role.

00:01:39.999 --> 00:01:42.234
And they said,
what about jailer?

00:01:42.234 --> 00:01:43.736
[ Groans ]

00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:46.672
I didn't know why I was being
asked about it,

00:01:46.672 --> 00:01:48.174
'cause, really,
everybody wants the big role.

00:01:48.174 --> 00:01:51.243
But now, I -- This is the
punishment for my own ego.

00:01:51.243 --> 00:01:53.680
I do every single one --
15,000 words.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:56.249
I'm playing everyone, if people
know "Great Expectations,"

00:01:56.249 --> 00:01:59.419
from Pip to Magwitch,
The Convict, to Estella,

00:01:59.419 --> 00:02:00.953
Miss Havisham,
I'm playing them all.

00:02:00.953 --> 00:02:04.257
And, so, I can only complain to
myself.

00:02:04.257 --> 00:02:05.257
But I love it. I do love it.

00:02:05.257 --> 00:02:06.426
And it is dramatic.

00:02:06.426 --> 00:02:08.394
There are certain parts
which are comedic.

00:02:08.394 --> 00:02:12.198
And tomorrow is my birthday.

00:02:12.198 --> 00:02:13.466
-Happy birthday!

00:02:13.466 --> 00:02:15.168
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:15.168 --> 00:02:20.973
-But, exciting, tomorrow is also
Charles Dickens' birthday.

00:02:20.973 --> 00:02:22.108
-Well.
-Yes.

00:02:22.108 --> 00:02:23.876
-Is this some sort of
cosmic --

00:02:23.876 --> 00:02:25.411
-It is a kind of cosmic.

00:02:25.411 --> 00:02:27.513
There's no word after cosmic.
Just a cosmic.

00:02:27.513 --> 00:02:31.216
And it's a -- I knew this from
when I was a kid.

00:02:31.216 --> 00:02:33.019
But I did the audio book.

00:02:33.019 --> 00:02:35.020
I got someone to pay me to do
the audio book

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:36.222
'cause I don't read,
I'm dyslexic,

00:02:36.222 --> 00:02:37.523
so I was a very slow reader.

00:02:37.523 --> 00:02:38.992
They paid me to read the book.

00:02:38.992 --> 00:02:41.794
So the audio book of "Great
expectations," 20 hours of it,

00:02:41.794 --> 00:02:43.896
is out there, and you can
download it at your pleasure.

00:02:43.896 --> 00:02:46.366
But I thought let's do a stage
show to go with it

00:02:46.366 --> 00:02:48.568
using the technique of
Richard Pryor.

00:02:48.568 --> 00:02:50.670
Which, Richard Pryor,
the great Richard Pryor,

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.538
in his stand-up he would
occasionally have characters

00:02:52.538 --> 00:02:54.440
going, hey, what do you think?
[ Mumbles ]

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.308
And they would turn to
each other and talk.

00:02:56.308 --> 00:02:58.077
And that's what I did throughout
my stand-up

00:02:58.077 --> 00:02:59.412
thinking that's what
everyone did.

00:02:59.412 --> 00:03:01.113
A lot of stand-ups
don't do that, actually.

00:03:01.113 --> 00:03:03.449
-It's very hard.
You're one of my favorites.

00:03:03.449 --> 00:03:06.719
During that, I remember seeing
you live and realizing

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:08.854
how you were in the
body of stand-up

00:03:08.854 --> 00:03:11.424
doing what seemed like sketch
comedy almost.

00:03:11.424 --> 00:03:12.925
You felt like it was a
visualization

00:03:12.925 --> 00:03:14.927
of all these characters.
-And that's where I came from.

00:03:14.927 --> 00:03:17.463
I came from very Monty Python
influenced sketch comedy.

00:03:17.463 --> 00:03:19.699
Just playing one character with
this kind of --

00:03:19.699 --> 00:03:21.100
And then playing another
character

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:22.801
with this kind of voice
in a different sketch.

00:03:22.801 --> 00:03:26.439
You now, and then some other
voice and different physicality.

00:03:26.439 --> 00:03:28.473
But now they're all together
talking to each other in,

00:03:28.473 --> 00:03:29.842
like, a weird chess game.

00:03:29.842 --> 00:03:33.279
And in the Dickens, I sometimes
ad lib as Estella or Pip,

00:03:33.279 --> 00:03:34.380
or Magwitch.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:38.718
I make things up
in Dickensian speak.

00:03:38.718 --> 00:03:40.853
Which is little Easter eggs

00:03:40.853 --> 00:03:42.021
for people who really
know the script.

00:03:42.021 --> 00:03:43.522
No one could memorize
the script.

00:03:43.522 --> 00:03:46.459
-Have you ever, you know,
ad libed outside of the text

00:03:46.459 --> 00:03:48.360
and had someone
in the audience go, "Hey!"?

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:49.862
-No.

00:03:49.862 --> 00:03:51.965
'Cause I would go, "What?"

00:03:51.965 --> 00:03:53.498
"You said the words wrong."

00:03:53.498 --> 00:03:54.967
"Well, [ bleep ] you."

00:03:54.967 --> 00:03:56.202
[ Laughter ]

00:03:56.202 --> 00:03:58.171
No, there's a line -- there's
one which I like,

00:03:58.171 --> 00:04:00.139
which I'm not allowed to take
out because it's too funny.

00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:02.741
Which is Uncle Pumblechook, this
kind of pompous guy,

00:04:02.741 --> 00:04:04.610
Uncle Pumblechook was a large,
hard-breathing man.

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:06.179
Face like a fish,
dull, staring eyes

00:04:06.179 --> 00:04:08.381
with hair that stuck up
like tuffs on his head.

00:04:08.381 --> 00:04:09.915
That's the actual description.

00:04:09.915 --> 00:04:11.951
And I changed it to large,
hard-breathing man,

00:04:11.951 --> 00:04:13.486
face like a fish,
dull, staring eyes,

00:04:13.486 --> 00:04:16.422
and hair that had obviously been
arranged by a dying relative.

00:04:16.422 --> 00:04:17.790
[ Laughter ]

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:19.324
Which kills every time.

00:04:19.324 --> 00:04:22.428
And I don't even lean into it.
I just throw it away.

00:04:22.428 --> 00:04:24.763
And I'm not allowed to change it
'cause it's too funny.

00:04:24.763 --> 00:04:26.132
-Well, you know what?

00:04:26.132 --> 00:04:29.067
I always thought somebody could
have done a punch-up pass

00:04:29.067 --> 00:04:30.369
on Dickens, you know?

00:04:30.369 --> 00:04:33.706
Just add a few more jokes.
-Yeah, I know.

00:04:33.706 --> 00:04:35.574
-He loves the great comic
description of stuff,

00:04:35.574 --> 00:04:37.109
but he doesn't really go
for the line.

00:04:37.109 --> 00:04:39.312
I'm sometimes leaning into his
lines to make them

00:04:39.312 --> 00:04:41.347
pop out a bit more, but now I'm
actually taking that away

00:04:41.347 --> 00:04:43.048
'cause it's the drama
that I love.

00:04:43.048 --> 00:04:44.917
You know, people think,
oh, yeah, you can do comedy.

00:04:44.917 --> 00:04:47.320
But the dramatic ending...
Pip is desperate.

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:49.088
He loves this girl, Estella,

00:04:49.088 --> 00:04:50.756
but she just doesn't
care for him.

00:04:50.756 --> 00:04:52.825
And it never happens for him.
It's unrequited love.

00:04:52.825 --> 00:04:54.026
I've been through that.

00:04:54.026 --> 00:04:55.527
A lot of us in the world
have been through that.

00:04:55.527 --> 00:04:57.430
Some of the very pretty people
don't go through that.

00:04:57.430 --> 00:04:58.864
[ Laughter ]

00:04:58.864 --> 00:05:02.101
But they're all bastards.

00:05:02.101 --> 00:05:05.938
-So you had your brother
actually adapted this.

00:05:05.938 --> 00:05:09.208
And, you know,
this is sort of a bridging

00:05:09.208 --> 00:05:11.510
of this very long, lengthy work.

00:05:11.510 --> 00:05:13.512
Is this something,
a skill he had?

00:05:13.512 --> 00:05:16.949
Was he surprised when you
came to him with this?

00:05:16.949 --> 00:05:18.083
-He's probably
more creative than me.

00:05:18.083 --> 00:05:19.451
He's a cartoonist and a writer.

00:05:19.451 --> 00:05:20.852
He wrote a book
when he was in his 20s.

00:05:20.852 --> 00:05:22.654
And then,
my older brother, Mark,

00:05:22.654 --> 00:05:25.424
he decided that he
wasn't gonna go after that.

00:05:25.424 --> 00:05:29.395
And, so, I said to him on this,
I was trying to lure him in.

00:05:29.395 --> 00:05:30.630
'Cause I know he's creative,
and I said,

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:32.164
should we adapt it together?

00:05:32.164 --> 00:05:33.933
'Cause I was writing my own
scripts at this point.

00:05:33.933 --> 00:05:35.034
And he said, yeah, okay.

00:05:35.034 --> 00:05:36.735
And then he said,
I've done the first part.

00:05:36.735 --> 00:05:38.270
There's three parts, I've done
the first part,

00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:39.539
which is quite a chunk of work.

00:05:39.539 --> 00:05:40.839
And then he said, now I've
done the second part.

00:05:40.839 --> 00:05:42.742
And, so,
I just let him go do it.

00:05:42.742 --> 00:05:43.876
And now he's done "Hamlet."

00:05:43.876 --> 00:05:46.912
"Hamlet" is also on the --
in waiting.

00:05:46.912 --> 00:05:48.948
I'm gonna do
a one-woman Hamlet as well.

00:05:48.948 --> 00:05:51.183
-That's very exciting.
-So, uh, these are just there --

00:05:51.183 --> 00:05:53.052
And tomorrow on my birthday, I'm
doing an extra gig

00:05:53.052 --> 00:05:55.421
at the JP Morgan's library.

00:05:55.421 --> 00:05:57.723
John Pierpont Morgan,
the great --

00:05:57.723 --> 00:05:59.525
well, not the great --
I don't know, great is --

00:05:59.525 --> 00:06:03.128
Conjecture there.
-Top five Morgans. Top five.

00:06:03.128 --> 00:06:06.365
I'm not sure what he was like a
person, but he left his house.

00:06:06.365 --> 00:06:08.234
He didn't stay holding onto it
when he was dead.

00:06:08.234 --> 00:06:09.335
He let it go.

00:06:09.335 --> 00:06:11.804
And I'm in his library tomorrow
night.

00:06:11.804 --> 00:06:14.473
After my show I'm doing
an extra show unplugged.

00:06:14.473 --> 00:06:16.976
Just me doing the verbals.
No lights and stuff.

00:06:16.976 --> 00:06:18.877
-Another thing you're
brother helped you out with

00:06:18.877 --> 00:06:20.246
that I was obviously
aware you did this.

00:06:20.246 --> 00:06:21.714
I did not realize
he was your translator.

00:06:21.714 --> 00:06:24.717
Another thing that I've been in
awe of is that you will do

00:06:24.717 --> 00:06:28.053
your stand-up act in
other languages.

00:06:28.053 --> 00:06:37.263
[ Speaking foreign languages ]

00:06:37.263 --> 00:06:40.233
Yeah, and encourage anyone,

00:06:40.233 --> 00:06:41.800
any stand-up,
particularly stand-ups

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:44.036
from all around the world --
Do it in a second language.

00:06:44.036 --> 00:06:45.371
It's tough,
but particularly in English.

00:06:45.371 --> 00:06:46.572
Anyone who's got another
language,

00:06:46.572 --> 00:06:48.373
if you do it in English,
you can tour the world.

00:06:48.373 --> 00:06:50.710
I have friends, French kid
called [ Inaudible ]

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:52.912
and he played in
Helsinki in Finland.

00:06:52.912 --> 00:06:55.081
And he was a French guy
talking in English.

00:06:55.081 --> 00:06:56.816
And the Finnish guys were
listening in English

00:06:56.816 --> 00:06:58.317
and laughing, and it works.

00:06:58.317 --> 00:07:00.686
It's a bridging.
English is a bridging language.

00:07:00.686 --> 00:07:03.155
It's not our language --
British, English --

00:07:03.155 --> 00:07:05.557
It's not yours.
It's not the Australians.

00:07:05.557 --> 00:07:07.026
It's not the
English-speaking Canadians.

00:07:07.026 --> 00:07:10.896
It is an open source language
just like the computer thing.

00:07:10.896 --> 00:07:12.598
Anyone can use English just --

00:07:12.598 --> 00:07:15.668
As we do around the world to
communicate business,

00:07:15.668 --> 00:07:16.969
to help medicine, you know.

00:07:16.969 --> 00:07:19.572
English is an easy language
to grab hold of.

00:07:19.572 --> 00:07:21.741
-With comedy, though, there must
be situations

00:07:21.741 --> 00:07:23.742
where you have to change --
-No.

00:07:23.742 --> 00:07:25.945
-To do a direct translation
is working against you.

00:07:25.945 --> 00:07:27.612
-No, it's only the references.

00:07:27.612 --> 00:07:30.583
Make sure you say KitKat.

00:07:30.583 --> 00:07:34.019
'Cause KitKats are
around the world.

00:07:34.019 --> 00:07:35.754
You know,
if I hit them with a KitKat,

00:07:35.754 --> 00:07:38.557
then they're, oh a KitKat,
that's a little biscuit thing.

00:07:38.557 --> 00:07:40.659
But if you say with a Twinkie,
they go --

00:07:40.659 --> 00:07:43.695
Twinkies, I'm afraid,
didn't get outside --

00:07:43.695 --> 00:07:46.432
-Oh, so you have to find
universal products.

00:07:46.432 --> 00:07:47.766
-Yeah, universal comedy.

00:07:47.766 --> 00:07:50.369
But if you talk about human
sacrifice, everyone know.

00:07:50.369 --> 00:07:51.938
[ Laughter ]

00:07:51.938 --> 00:07:56.442
You say, basically, human
sacrifice was,

00:07:56.442 --> 00:08:00.946
okay, the crops are bad,
the Gods obviously hate us,

00:08:00.946 --> 00:08:02.748
so we're gonna kill Steve.

00:08:02.748 --> 00:08:05.585
And then it will probably
pick up next year.

00:08:05.585 --> 00:08:07.753
And Steve is going, why me?

00:08:07.753 --> 00:08:09.822
Well, Steve,
I never did like you,

00:08:09.822 --> 00:08:11.924
and I'm the witch doctor with
the bone through the nose.

00:08:11.924 --> 00:08:13.392
And that's how that works.

00:08:13.392 --> 00:08:16.328
You talk about human sacrifice,
they get in it Russia, in China,

00:08:16.328 --> 00:08:18.164
they get it in Istanbul.

00:08:18.164 --> 00:08:19.731
It doesn't matter where you are,

00:08:19.731 --> 00:08:22.735
people understand what you're
talking about, human sacrifice.

00:08:22.735 --> 00:08:24.470
It's just a thing that every
country understands

00:08:24.470 --> 00:08:26.538
that thousands of years ago,
we were all idiots,

00:08:26.538 --> 00:08:28.674
and all doing it.
Being controlled.

00:08:28.674 --> 00:08:31.677
-I mean, this is -- I do think
in general, you talk about,

00:08:31.677 --> 00:08:34.747
sorry, excuse me,
themes that are universal.

00:08:34.747 --> 00:08:38.450
Things like the universe,
religion, like humanity.

00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:40.418
Was that where you started
as stand-up?

00:08:40.418 --> 00:08:42.788
With the big ideas?
Or did you work your way there?

00:08:42.788 --> 00:08:44.890
-Well, you know, if you start
with the big ideas,

00:08:44.890 --> 00:08:46.792
People go, boring.
-Yeah.

00:08:46.792 --> 00:08:48.994
I actually have a theory of the
universe.

00:08:48.994 --> 00:08:50.062
I've worked out how it works.

00:08:50.062 --> 00:08:52.932
And I could be wrong,
but I have --

00:08:52.932 --> 00:08:55.434
It's not a comedy version
of the theory.

00:08:55.434 --> 00:08:57.636
I was using it in my last show.

00:08:57.636 --> 00:08:59.437
It was basically that
everything is circular

00:08:59.437 --> 00:09:01.440
and the big bang, the
crunch, big bang, big crunch,

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:03.109
No Gods,
no one's controlling it,

00:09:03.109 --> 00:09:04.577
we're just lucky enough
to be here.

00:09:04.577 --> 00:09:06.345
Meaning of life, there is no
meaning,

00:09:06.345 --> 00:09:07.880
but some of us put meaning
into life

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:09.682
and some of us take it
out of life.

00:09:09.682 --> 00:09:13.318
So, this was my thing, but there
was no jokes in it, initially.

00:09:13.318 --> 00:09:15.554
So I had to put in some jokes
along the way

00:09:15.554 --> 00:09:17.256
of me trying to say
this is my theory

00:09:17.256 --> 00:09:19.191
on how the universe works.

00:09:19.191 --> 00:09:22.561
-I think, in a very good way,
comedy has become a place where

00:09:22.561 --> 00:09:23.896
you can have a big idea,

00:09:23.896 --> 00:09:26.332
and then if you make sure
to put jokes into it,

00:09:26.332 --> 00:09:29.935
people are willing to stand
around and be explained.

00:09:29.935 --> 00:09:31.704
They like having a few jokes
to go with it.

00:09:31.704 --> 00:09:33.205
-I call it comedy philosophy.

00:09:33.205 --> 00:09:35.774
Yeah, it's like we can be
comic philosophers if we wish.

00:09:35.774 --> 00:09:37.710
We can say, what is it?
How does it work?

00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:39.712
How do religions work?
Is one of them right?

00:09:39.712 --> 00:09:41.013
Is the other one right?

00:09:41.013 --> 00:09:43.248
Is a god gonna come down and
say I'm the God Ping?

00:09:43.248 --> 00:09:46.318
And I believe that all soot
should be saluted?

00:09:46.318 --> 00:09:48.187
That's it, you know?

00:09:48.187 --> 00:09:50.356
And you go, oh, I thought --
what about the other god?

00:09:50.356 --> 00:09:51.556
No, they're wrong.
It's just me.

00:09:51.556 --> 00:09:53.391
And then we go, what?

00:09:53.391 --> 00:09:55.327
You could talk about God 'cause
every religion

00:09:55.327 --> 00:09:56.562
to the next religion
looks crazy.

00:09:56.562 --> 00:09:57.863
And I don't believe
in any of them.

00:09:57.863 --> 00:09:59.264
I just believe in human beings.

00:09:59.264 --> 00:10:00.999
-There you go, that's a pretty
good thing to believe in.

00:10:00.999 --> 00:10:03.535
-Human beings are here,
and I can get in with them.

00:10:03.535 --> 00:10:05.270
-Do you remember --

00:10:05.270 --> 00:10:07.940
What was your first,
what was your first show

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:09.241
in the states?

00:10:09.241 --> 00:10:13.579
-First one was, I did the
Memphis in May festival in 1988.

00:10:13.579 --> 00:10:14.647
-In Memphis, Tennessee?

00:10:14.647 --> 00:10:16.082
-In Memphis Tennessee in
Overton square,

00:10:16.082 --> 00:10:18.517
If anyone's in Overton Square.
I actually went back there.

00:10:18.517 --> 00:10:20.586
I said I began here
and no one cared.

00:10:20.586 --> 00:10:23.589
I did my first show --
-No statue? No plaque?

00:10:23.589 --> 00:10:26.625
-I was building. Hammering in --

00:10:26.625 --> 00:10:28.260
Get that thing out of the way.

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:30.930
It was a car park
and now it's [ Inaudible ]

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:33.299
But it's a really good, anyway,
I did it there,

00:10:33.299 --> 00:10:34.833
and I said I could
go back to London.

00:10:34.833 --> 00:10:38.337
Maybe I can go via New York.
'cause obviously this thing

00:10:38.337 --> 00:10:40.372
of being in New York,
and I could worked it out,

00:10:40.372 --> 00:10:42.507
I could pay them some money
and they said,

00:10:42.507 --> 00:10:44.777
Washington Square park is where
the street performers perform.

00:10:44.777 --> 00:10:46.478
I was a street performer for
four years

00:10:46.478 --> 00:10:48.881
and that gives me
a special, weird ability

00:10:48.881 --> 00:10:50.816
to perform out
when no one's expecting it.

00:10:50.816 --> 00:10:54.219
And in the fountain of
Washington Square Park,

00:10:54.219 --> 00:10:56.322
that's the first ever show
I did in 1988.

00:10:56.322 --> 00:10:58.924
I made about $20,
and I was pleased.

00:10:58.924 --> 00:11:02.127
-Well, 20 bucks in 1988,
that's, like, $28 now.

00:11:02.127 --> 00:11:05.096
-Exactly, and it's
really kicked up.

00:11:05.096 --> 00:11:07.466
But also, I could tell
everyone back in London

00:11:07.466 --> 00:11:10.503
I've performed in New York.

00:11:10.503 --> 00:11:12.537
No one ever sees you doing gigs.

00:11:12.537 --> 00:11:14.306
I was very funny, too.

00:11:14.306 --> 00:11:17.843
-Do you think that doing those
as a street performer,

00:11:17.843 --> 00:11:21.847
ultimately, any show you do now
is so much easier

00:11:21.847 --> 00:11:23.582
because you do have
people's attention?

00:11:23.582 --> 00:11:26.184
-Yes. Yes, it is.
But also they demand, you know?

00:11:26.184 --> 00:11:29.154
The audience, when you are on
the street, can say,

00:11:29.154 --> 00:11:30.489
well, this isn't very good.
We're going.

00:11:30.489 --> 00:11:32.791
But they don't
throw things at you.

00:11:32.791 --> 00:11:34.726
Not that most audiences throw
things at you.

00:11:34.726 --> 00:11:38.597
But it's easier when
they're there.

00:11:38.597 --> 00:11:41.767
The French audience and the
German audience are very polite.

00:11:41.767 --> 00:11:43.635
And the Spanish was, actually.

00:11:43.635 --> 00:11:44.969
The British ones are not.

00:11:44.969 --> 00:11:51.376
[ Mimicking audience ]

00:11:51.376 --> 00:11:52.645
You know, this kind of stuff.

00:11:52.645 --> 00:11:54.246
I don't know if that happens
in New York as well.

00:11:54.246 --> 00:11:55.814
-They call you a cab from stage?

00:11:55.814 --> 00:11:57.882
-Yeah. They say, "Cab for Eddie
Izzard," you know.

00:11:57.882 --> 00:12:00.385
That was the big joke.

00:12:00.385 --> 00:12:01.687
"Say something funny."

00:12:01.687 --> 00:12:03.856
I knew I was getting good when
somebody heckled me once

00:12:03.856 --> 00:12:06.524
and said, "say something funny,"
and I went, "No."

00:12:06.524 --> 00:12:08.026
And then just carried on.

00:12:08.026 --> 00:12:11.163
And I go, whoa, okay,
my confidence is high there.

00:12:11.163 --> 00:12:14.699
I used to do these elaborate
putdowns and heckles.

00:12:14.699 --> 00:12:17.469
Someone heckles,
I'd say, this guy is Kenny.

00:12:17.469 --> 00:12:19.204
He's having a lot of
problems at the moment.

00:12:19.204 --> 00:12:21.540
His doctor said come along
and shout at me.

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:22.408
Well done, Kenny.

00:12:22.408 --> 00:12:24.609
You shouted out, "Go away,"

00:12:24.609 --> 00:12:26.044
but used more of
the swear words there.

00:12:26.044 --> 00:12:29.849
And make it into a sentence,
but keep going.

00:12:29.849 --> 00:12:32.017
In five minutes he will be
heckling again.

00:12:32.017 --> 00:12:34.253
Well done, Kenny.
We're with you.

00:12:34.253 --> 00:12:36.488
And you impose scenarios on him.

00:12:36.488 --> 00:12:39.658
Any stand-up out there can use
this, well, yeah, use it.

00:12:39.658 --> 00:12:41.760
It's a technique,
just impose scenarios.

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:44.530
And then everyone thinks, they
know it's not Kenny, but

00:12:44.530 --> 00:12:46.198
they go, "It's Kenny again."

00:12:46.198 --> 00:12:48.333
"What was that?
That wasn't even a sentence.

00:12:48.333 --> 00:12:49.601
You're going backwards, Kenny."

00:12:49.601 --> 00:12:51.771
It's a lovely little
technique to use

00:12:51.771 --> 00:12:53.305
when people are
shouting things out.

00:12:53.305 --> 00:12:55.207
-Well, it is so lovely
to have you here.

00:12:55.207 --> 00:12:58.076
Thank you so much,
and always a pleasure.

00:12:58.076 --> 00:12:59.344
That's Eddie Izzard, everybody.

00:12:59.344 --> 00:13:01.647
"Great Expectations"
playing at

00:13:01.647 --> 00:13:03.415
The Greenwich Theater
through February 11th.

00:13:03.415 --> 00:13:04.884
We'll be right back with
more "Late Night."

